<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Grão-Pará and Rio Negro</h1><p class="st-meta">1772 CE – 1775 CE · capital Belém</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived Portuguese administrative state covering the Amazon basin, governed from Belém after the division of Grão-Pará and Maranhão.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1772 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-grao-para-and-maranhao">State of Grão-Pará and Maranhão</a> was split in two. The western part, comprising the Captaincy of Grão-Pará and the Captaincy of São José do <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-negro">Rio Negro</a>, created in 1755 with its seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelos-amazonas">Barcelos</a>, became the State of Grão-Pará and Rio Negro, governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belem">Belém</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The state covered most of the Brazilian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a>. Its economy relied on forest products such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloves">cloves</a> and sarsaparilla gathered by indigenous workers under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diretorio-dos-indios">Directorate</a> system, and on trade through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belem">Belém</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>. Portuguese forts along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-negro">Rio Negro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/branco-river">Rio Branco</a> guarded the frontier with Spanish territories.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Sources differ on how long the separate state lasted, and its captaincies were gradually brought under the general government of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a>. Grão-Pará remained loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> at independence and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Empire of Brazil</a> only in 1823. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cabanagem">Cabanagem</a> revolt shook the region from 1835 to 1840.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-grao-para-and-rio-negro.jpg" alt="State of Grão-Pará and Rio Negro" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan This W3C-unspecified vector image was created with Inkscape . · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Portugal_(1750).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1772 CE – 1775 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Belém</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese, Nheengatu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Estado do Grão-Pará e Rio Negro</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1774&amp;focus=state-of-grao-para-and-rio-negro" aria-label="State of Grão-Pará and Rio Negro on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3333 2232 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Grão-Pará and Rio Negro lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3653 2488L3653 2488M3653 2488L3944 2917"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3653 2488h0M3944 2917h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3653 2488h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3653 2488h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Belém. <a href="/atlas?year=1774&amp;focus=state-of-grao-para-and-rio-negro">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
